Never block the wayland event-reader thread on a drag-and-drop transfer read.

read_all() did a plain blocking read() with no timeout, and it runs on the
event-reader thread: data_device_enter -> dnd_build_entries -> import_format ->
read_all. That thread is the ONLY one that drains the compositor socket, so
while it is blocked NO wl_buffer.release is read for ANY surface: every window's
SHM buffer queue starves at once and the UI thread livelocks in
wayland_buffer_queue_get_free_buffer(), waiting 100 ms per frame for a release
that can never arrive because nothing is reading it.

Measured on a live stall (Premiere, 2026-08-13, after a timeline drag-and-drop):
`ss -xp` showed Recv-Q = 7768 bytes sent by a healthy, actively-compositing KWin
and never read, while the app sat at ~0% CPU with 3 windows starving
simultaneously and ~10 failed buffer acquisitions per second. The UI was dead for
roughly five minutes before something timed out and it recovered.

The fd is a pipe whose write end we hand to the DRAG SOURCE, so a slow, wedged or
uncooperative source stalls our entire rendering pipeline. Worse, when the source
is the same process -- dragging from Premiere's project panel onto its timeline --
the thread that must write can be the very UI thread that is starving, which is an
outright self-deadlock.

Poll with a bounded budget (2 s of total silence) and give up rather than block.
A partial payload is discarded, not returned: a truncated uri-list would drop the
wrong file, and failing a transfer is better than corrupting it.

Note the do/while: nread must be initialised, because the timeout path `continue`s
straight to the `while (nread > 0)` test.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_data_device.c	2026-08-13 02:22:35.478060224 -0400
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_data_device.c	2026-08-13 02:22:35.466477159 -0400
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
+#include <poll.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 
@@ -121,12 +122,20 @@
     }
 }
 
+/* 🚨 NEUTRON: total time read_all() may spend waiting for the OTHER side of the
+ * transfer pipe. See the comment there. */
+#define NEUTRON_DND_READ_TIMEOUT_MS 2000
+
 static void *read_all(int fd, size_t *size_out)
 {
     size_t buffer_size = 4096;
     int total = 0;
     unsigned char *buffer;
-    int nread;
+    /* ⚠️ MUST start non-zero: the poll-timeout path below uses `continue`, which
+     * in a do/while jumps straight to the `while (nread > 0)` test -- reading it
+     * uninitialised on the very first iteration is undefined behaviour. */
+    int nread = 1;
+    int waited_ms = 0;
 
     if (!(buffer = malloc(buffer_size)))
     {
@@ -136,6 +145,53 @@
 
     do
     {
+        /* 🚨 NEUTRON: NEVER block indefinitely here. This runs on the wayland
+         * EVENT-READER THREAD (data_device_enter -> dnd_build_entries ->
+         * import_format -> here), and that thread is the ONLY one that drains
+         * the compositor socket: while it is blocked, no wl_buffer.release is
+         * read for ANY surface, so every window's SHM buffer queue starves at
+         * once and the app's UI thread livelocks in
+         * wayland_buffer_queue_get_free_buffer(). Premiere froze for ~5 minutes
+         * that way after a timeline drag-and-drop (2026-08-13); measured during
+         * a live stall with `ss -xp`: Recv-Q = 7768 bytes sent by a healthy
+         * compositor and never read.
+         *
+         * The fd is a pipe whose write end we handed to the DRAG SOURCE. A slow,
+         * wedged, or uncooperative source therefore hangs our whole rendering
+         * pipeline -- and when the source is the SAME process (dragging from
+         * Premiere's project panel onto its timeline) the thread that must write
+         * can be the very UI thread that is starving, which is an outright
+         * self-deadlock.
+         *
+         * Bail out after a bounded wait and return what we have (or nothing):
+         * a drag that fails to deliver is a bad outcome, a frozen app is a far
+         * worse one. */
+        struct pollfd pfd = { .fd = fd, .events = POLLIN };
+        int pr = poll(&pfd, 1, 50);
+
+        if (pr == 0)
+        {
+            waited_ms += 50;
+            if (waited_ms >= NEUTRON_DND_READ_TIMEOUT_MS)
+            {
+                ERR("neutron-dnd: transfer source sent nothing in %d ms; abandoning the read "
+                    "rather than blocking the wayland event thread (that starves every "
+                    "surface's buffer queue and freezes the app)\n", waited_ms);
+                /* Discard any PARTIAL payload: a truncated uri-list would drop the
+                 * wrong file. Failing the transfer is better than corrupting it. */
+                total = 0;
+                goto out;
+            }
+            continue;
+        }
+        if (pr == -1)
+        {
+            if (errno == EINTR) continue;
+            TRACE("poll failed on transfer fd (errno: %d)\n", errno);
+            total = 0;
+            goto out;
+        }
+
         nread = read(fd, buffer + total, buffer_size - total);
         if (nread == -1 && errno != EINTR)
         {
